   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>S’intende dovrebbe esser <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                            <foreign xml:lang="ell">εχθρος</foreign> non <foreign xml:lang="ell">εκτρος</foreign>
                        </seg>. Ma quale accento?</p> <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">J.J. </name>
                        </signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
                <div type="translation">
                    <p>It’s understood it should be εχθρος not εκτρος. But what accent?</p> <closer>
                        <signed>J.J.</signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">This postcard shows a color photograph captioned “Zurichsee und Glärnischgruppe vom Uetliberg Aus,” that is, Lake Zurich and the Glärnisch mountain range in the Swiss Alps, viewed from Uetliberg, a mountain and prominent viewpoint to the west of Zurich. The photograph is attributed to “J. Gaberell,” or J. Gaberell A.G. (1923-1949), a company in Zurich that published photographs of Jean Gaberell (1887-1949), best known for his views of the Alps and figures engaged in winter sports.</note>
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">εχθρός means “enemy” or “foe”, both in ancient and modern Greek. JJ does not use any accent marks here. At the time, JJ was assisting Frank Budgen with the book he was writing. In his account of their visit to Locarno, Budgen reproduces the Greek word, its first letter bearing accent marks (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">JJMU</name> 247). Ruggiero must have obliged and supplied them. Thanks to Stephanie Nelson for assistance with the Greek.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
